mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — strategies that make real choices\n\nAbbreviated strategies across business types. The point of each: it **chooses** — names the\nchannels it will win on *and the ones it drops*, commits to one objective, and has a wedge. Each\nis contrasted with the \"be everywhere\" non-strategy. (Illustrative.)\n\n---\n\n## 1. B2B SaaS — payroll tool for small-business owners\n- **Objective:** leads (demo sign-ups) + authority. Metric: demo requests + DMs (manual).\n- **Channels:** **LinkedIn** (primary, founder-led) + **X** (secondary). **Drop:** TikTok,\n  Pinterest, Instagram, YouTube long-form.\n- **Why:** the audience (owner-operators, and the practitioners who follow) is on LinkedIn/X; the\n  brand's strength is written insight, not video; LinkedIn fits the leads/authority goal.\n- **Wedge:** radical transparency + the \"payroll without a specialist\" POV — saying what bloated\n  competitors won't.\n- **Cadence:** LinkedIn 3×/week (founder), X ~daily, repurposed from the same atoms.\n- *Non-strategy (avoid):* \"Be on all 10 platforms and post daily.\" → spreads a small team thin,\n  wins nowhere, and forces video the brand can't sustain.\n\n## 2. Local — neighborhood coffee shop\n- **Objective:** local awareness + community. Metric: saves, story replies, \"saw it on IG\"\n  mentions (manual).\n- **Channels:** **Instagram** (primary) + **Facebook** (secondary — older/local + Groups).\n  **Drop:** LinkedIn, X, TikTok (unless the owner enjoys it).\n- **Why:** locals are on IG/FB; the brand's strength is warm visual moments; goal is local reach,\n  not B2B.\n- **Wedge:** the human, neighborhood angle — the owner's real voice, regulars by name.\n- **Cadence:** IG ~4×/week, FB cross-posted/adapted.\n\n## 3. Ecommerce / DTC — sustainable sneakers\n- **Objective:** awareness → sales. Metric: link clicks + sales (native/manual).\n- **Channels:** **Instagram** + **TikTok** (primary — visual/video) + **Pinterest** (evergreen\n  search traffic). **Drop:** LinkedIn.\n- **Why:** the audience discovers products on IG/TikTok; Pinterest drives evergreen buying intent;\n  the brand has strong visual content.\n- **Wedge:** design-first sustainability — looks good first, eco is the bonus.\n- **Cadence:** IG/TikTok several×/week (much repurposed), Pinterest steady evergreen pins.\n\n## 4. Personal brand — indie maker\n- **Objective:** audience growth + authority. Metric: followers *that engage* + email sign-ups.\n- **Channels:** **X** (primary) + **LinkedIn** (secondary). **Drop:** the rest, at least in\n  Phase 1.\n- **Why:** builders live on X; the brand's strength is written, candid insight; one person's\n  capacity suits one or two text-first channels.\n- **Wedge:** build-in-public with *real numbers* — honesty as the moat.\n- **Cadence:** X daily-ish, LinkedIn 2–3×/week repurposed.\n\n---\n\n## The pattern across all four\n\n- Each names a **single primary objective** tied to the business.\n- Each **picks 1–3 channels by fit** and **explicitly drops the rest** — that's the strategic act.\n- Each channel choice matches the **audience** *and* the brand's **content strength**.\n- Each has a **wedge** from its POV — a reason it wins, not just \"post good content.\"\n- Cadence is **sustainable** and leans on **repurposing** to cover multiple channels.\n\nA strategy that doesn't drop anything isn't choosing — and a strategy that doesn't choose isn't a\nstrategy.\n"
